<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bulgars</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Bulgar</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Bulgars were a Turkic-speaking nomadic people of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pontic-caspian-steppe">Pontic-Caspian steppe</a> who emerged in the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">medieval period</a>. Some founded the First Bulgarian state on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> in the seventh century, while others settled on the middle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volga">Volga</a>. Steppe peoples such as the Bulgars and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khazar-khaganate">Khazars</a> inherited political traditions shaped by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gokturk-khaganate">Gokturk Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bulgars.jpg" alt="Bulgars" width="320" height="156" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Original: Constantine Manasses · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:53-manasses-chronicle.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Bulgars were Turkic semi-nomadic warrior tribes of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pontic-caspian-steppe">Pontic-Caspian steppe</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volga">Volga</a> region. Their original homeland is unclear, but is believed to lie in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kazakhstan">Kazakhstan</a> and the North Caucasian steppes. Some scholars propose that they were a branch of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huns">Huns</a> or absorbed them, a question still debated. Because of the lack of definitive evidence, modern scholarship explains their origin as ethnogenesis, in which warrior elites ruled a variety of groups that adopted a shared name. The first clear mention of the Bulgars is from 480, when they were allies of the Byzantine Emperor Zeno against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ostrogoths">Ostrogoths</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-turkic-khaganate">Western Turkic Khaganate</a> declined in the middle of the 7th century, the Bulgars revolted against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avars">Avars</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kubrat">Kubrat</a>, around 635, and founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/old-great-bulgaria">Old Great Bulgaria</a>. It proved a loose tribal union, and a rivalry with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khazar-khaganate">Khazars</a> ended in Khazar victory in 668. The union broke up. Some Bulgars stayed in the Pontic steppe as Khazar vassals, and others, led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kotrag">Kotrag</a>, moved to the middle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volga">Volga</a> and founded Volga Bulgaria with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolghar">Bolghar</a> as capital.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asparuh-of-bulgaria">Asparukh</a>, a third son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kubrat">Kubrat</a> according to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nikephoros-i">Nikephoros I</a>, crossed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> and in 681 conquered Scythia Minor, establishing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-bulgarian-empire">First Bulgarian Empire</a>, where the Bulgars became a political and military elite. They merged with the Byzantine populations and Slavic tribes there and were in time Slavicized, becoming one of the ancestors of modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgarians">Bulgarians</a>. The Volga Bulgars accepted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> in 922, repelled the first Mongol attacks in 1223 and were subdued by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> in 1237.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The Bulgars shared the culture of the nomadic horsemen of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a>, moving seasonally in search of pasture while trading with settled societies, from whom they learned blacksmithing, pottery and carpentry. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkans">Balkans</a> they quickly adopted the sedentary life of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavs">Slavs</a>. The Danubian Bulgars had a clan and military system of inner and outer tribes under a ruling clan, whose ruler's title was khan.</p><p>Little is known of their religion, but it is believed to have been monotheistic, centred on the Turkic sky deity Tengri, known to them as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tangra">Tangra</a>. Their elite spoke a Turkic language of the Oghur branch, the only surviving member of which is Chuvash. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volga-tatars">Volga Tatars</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bashkirs">Bashkirs</a> and Chuvash are said to descend from the Volga Bulgars.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Bulgars” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>At its top stood a mounted warrior elite of steppe origin; beneath it lived Gepids, Romanised provincials, Kutrigurs, <b>Bulgars</b> and, above all, Slavs, who formed much of the population and provided farmers and auxiliary troops.</blockquote><a href="/empire/avar-khaganate">Avar Khaganate · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Bulgars</b> were a Turkic-speaking steppe people who in the early 7th century formed the confederation known as Old Great Bulgaria under Khan Kubrat, north of the Black Sea.</blockquote><a href="/empire/first-bulgarian-empire">First Bulgarian Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The Hungarians, beaten on the Pontic steppes around 895 by <b>Bulgars</b> and Pechenegs, were moving into the Carpathian Basin.</blockquote><a href="/empire/great-moravia">Great Moravia · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>In 469 Dengizich was defeated and killed in Thrace, and the Huns were absorbed by peoples such as the <b>Bulgars</b>; Kim argues they lived on under Ernak as the Kutrigur and Utigur Hunno-Bulgars.</blockquote><a href="/empire/hunnic-empire">Hunnic Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Khudyakov described the khanate as formed from an older <b>Bulgar</b> base, with its population and culture, and a Tatar element that brought the state organisation.</blockquote><a href="/empire/khanate-of-kazan">Khanate of Kazan · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Turkic-speaking nomadic elites founded it and took control of the lands from the lower Volga to the northern Caucasus and the steppes north of the Black Sea, subjugating or displacing rivals such as the <b>Bulgars</b> of Kubrat's Old Great Bulgaria.</blockquote><a href="/empire/khazar-khaganate">Khazar Khaganate · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
